For UVa 10035 **"Primary Arithmetic"**, you only need to count how many carry operations occur when adding two numbers digit by digit.

Here's a simple student-style C program:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main()
{
    long long a, b;

    while (1)
    {
        scanf("%lld %lld", &a, &b);

        if (a == 0 && b == 0)
            break;

        int carry = 0;
        int count = 0;

        while (a > 0 || b > 0)
        {
            int d1 = a % 10;
            int d2 = b % 10;

            if (d1 + d2 + carry >= 10)
            {
                count++;
                carry = 1;
            }
            else
            {
                carry = 0;
            }

            a /= 10;
            b /= 10;
        }

        if (count == 0)
            printf("No carry operation.\n");
        else if (count == 1)
            printf("1 carry operation.\n");
        else
            printf("%d carry operations.\n", count);
    }

    return 0;
}
```

### Sample Input

```text
123 456
555 555
123 594
0 0
```

### Sample Output

```text
No carry operation.
3 carry operations.
1 carry operation.
```

### Idea

* Add digits from right to left.
* Keep track of the carry from the previous addition.
* Count every time the sum produces a carry.
* Print according to the required format:

  * `No carry operation.`
  * `1 carry operation.`
  * `x carry operations.`

This is the classic accepted solution for UVa 10035 and looks like a typical college student's competitive programming code.
